The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
void perf_callchain_user(struct perf_callchain_entry_ctx *entry, struct pt_regs *regs)
{
	if (!is_32bit_task())
		perf_callchain_user_64(entry, regs);
	else
		perf_callchain_user_32(entry, regs);
}
```
Reason:
The function calls architecture-specific callchain walk functions (`perf_callchain_user_64` or `perf_callchain_user_32`) without first checking whether `current->mm` is NULL. During process exit or memory release, `current->mm` can become NULL, causing those functions to attempt reading user memory through an invalid descriptor, leading to a kernel NULL pointer dereference panic.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
void perf_callchain_user(struct perf_callchain_entry_ctx *entry, struct pt_regs *regs)
{
	perf_callchain_store(entry, perf_arch_instruction_pointer(regs));

	if (!current->mm)
		return;

	if (!is_32bit_task())
		perf_callchain_user_64(entry, regs);
	else
		perf_callchain_user_32(entry, regs);
}
```
Reason:
The added `if (!current->mm) return;` check prevents calling the architecture-specific walk functions when the memory descriptor is NULL, avoiding the panic. Moving `perf_callchain_store` before the check ensures that the top-level instruction pointer is always recorded in the callchain, even if `current->mm` is NULL.

